Date:     Fri, 8 Jan 93 9:24:41 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Jevans' false dichotomy - Markets v. Planning

One of David Jevans' claims was that external costs were market failures.  He
may not have put in in those terms, but that's how I construed it.  He
called them "global effects," and listed such (cliche) examples as pollution.

I don't mean to belittle the concern underlying this, but that, combined with
his claim that leaving such things to the interaction of agents economists
call "markets" means that planning is futile is simply false.

Frankly, I think much of this impression is due to the way that the term
"market" seems familiar, but it used as a term of art by economists in an
unfamiliar way.  The Austrian economists prefer the term "catallaxy," which
I'll follow their useage of.

Catallaxies don't preclude individual planning, they preclude "collective"
planning, also called central planning.  They rely on individual planning
for growth.

External costs come in two categories: invasions of defined property rights,
and cases in which property rights aren't defined or assigned.  Most of
our "environmental problems" are the latter.  The answer here isn't to
adopt central planning, but to define and assign property rights.  Pollution
ought to be treated in law as a tresspass, and is to some extent.

