From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Re:  Jevans' false dichotomy - Markets v. Planning

I would like to expand on Tim Starrs posting.  Air pollution is a
'commons' problem.  Each of us can pollute a little with a small
impact on the overall quality of air in the world, but if everyone
does it, the air gets very dirty.  

Imagine, instead that each area owned the air above it's land.
For example, if I lived downwind of a pollution source, I could
sue them for dirtying MY air.  

Another example would be a regional air authority that excercised
air management over an area.  It would set a pollution rate 
to charge polluters that compensated property owners in the
area.  A pollutor outside the air authority region would have to
pay them when the wind blew in that direction.  Thus, those
who valued clean air more could have their air authority board
set a higher rate.  Then the pollutor could decide to store pollution
onsite till the wind changed direction, or scrub it's exhausts 
more.

These change the incentive structure by making pollution a cost,
hence something to be minimized.  If the air is a commons, the
cost to the pollutor is minimal.

