Date: Fri, 8 Jan 93 13:23:34 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Virtual Vision

The Wall Street Journal reported today (p. B1) on an exciting new consumer
electronics product:  the Virtual Vision Sport, made by Virtual Vision,
Inc.  

The VVS provides a headup video display through a 5 oz pair of glasses,
which look like bulky wraparound shades.  Video images are projected onto
the reflective inner side of the glasses' lenses, and the viewer sees them
as if they were projected on a transparent screen 8 to 15 ft away (the
apparent size of that virtual screen is not mentioned).  Realspace images
are visible through the VVS image (the article doesn't mention how readily
viewers can process both virtual and real images, but the VVS no doubt has
brightness controls that allow one to regulate which image comes in most
powerfully).  

The video signal enters the VVS glasses through an light wire attached to a
receiver unit (it's size and weight was not given, but it resembles a
portable CD unit).  The receiver picks up wireless TV signals and can also
process signals from VCRs and the like.  Though the article does not
explicitly refer to computer video signals, these would surely pose little
problem for the VVS.  The VVS should be available for around $900 in April
of 1993.

I am very curious about how the VVS will compare to the Private Eye that
Doug Platt uses for his Hip PC (and am thus sending a copy of this post
directly to his email address).  The VVS will probably offer higher
resolution.  The article doesn't mention whether or not it transmits color
images; if so, it would have another advantage over the monocrome Private
Eye.  The VVS may also be lighter.  It arguably looks a whole lot cooler,
too; it certainly is less obviously a VR device.  Cost could be a problem,
though I suspect that a good part of the VVS's $900 price tag is covering
the TV receiver.  The glasses alone may be a good bit cheaper.

I suppose I don't need to spell out all the possible uses of a VVS/computer
device; Extropians are quite familiar with all the exciting opportunities
offered by a relatively cheap, light, private, and unobtrusive video
output.  I'm personally looking forward to connecting my VVS glasses to a
motion detector and software that allows me to work on a virtual computer
desktop as broad as a room.  And that's just for starters . . . 8-)




