  Well, I don't recall anyone claiming that "history shows that evolution 
is space filling".  However, as regards weapons development, history is
fairly clear.  Is it possible that mankind will yet destroy itself?  Surely.
Is it becoming ever less likely?  I believe the facts support this
conclusion.  To wit, we have for the first time (in history) an era in which
the raw numbers, total throw weight and standby launchers for nuclear
weapons are decreasing.  Both sides in the former "Cold War" are stepping
down from an alert status that lasted for 40 years.  The real threat that
defense planners are addressing is now the mad man/nation with its single
or fistfull of nukes launching in a suicide strike.  Not that this would
comfort anyone at ground zero of a detonation, but the mass extermination of
humankind by this method is most unlikely.  

  But with the threat of nuclear annihilation dimming, new threats are on
the rise.  Most particularly, nanotech presents a clear opportunity for
autogenocide.  I am heartened, however, to see clear discussion of the 
risks and of possible containment strategies occuring *now*, while the
technology is only a gleam is our collective eye.  I take this as a sign
of (racial) maturity, mitigating (but not removing) the possibility that
we will destroy ourselves through nanotech.

  Your final point in this paragraph appears to be that we should not
expect to extrapolate from our own experience to speculate about what
other lifeforms might do, or think.  Surely the chances that our speculations
might be wrong are good, but I think speculation is yet fruitful.   One
reason why the arguments of Perry, et al, that other intelligences would
likely take over galaxies, etc. if they existed stems from a simple
observation about evolution, namely, that it is competitive.  That is, 
a successfully evolving organism succeeds by outcompeting other similar
organisms.  To speculate that other beings do not have the competitive
drive requires that you either explain how they evolved without it, or
to speculate that they learned to subdue it.  This leads me to my point:
Man is not unique in his urge to compete -- he is unique in two regards
1) He is a highly effective competitor, able to develop nuclear weapons
to aid in his competition, if neccessary, and 2) He is the only species
that (as yet) has shown the capacity to restrain his competition.  ETIs may
have the same capacity, in a racial sense, but how many ETI-sociopaths
does it take to unleash destruction on our fragile race?  I find the
arguments for our being alone, or at least the furthest "advanced" in
our section of the universe compelling.
