From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: TECH/POL: A Technology Power Dive?

A Technology Power Dive?

A jet that flames out can often only be restarted by doing the
counterintuitive thing: diving at high speed toward the ground and
hoping that the increased speed will allow the engines to ignite
again.

(To the purists, a flamed-out engine can't give a "power" dive, but
whatever the dive may be called, I think of it as a power dive.)

I've often thought this is an interesting metaphor for the situation
we are in today, especially with regard to energy policy, fossil fuel
consumption trends, and new technologies such as self-reproducing
systems, nanotech, viruses, biotech, etc.

We haven't exactly "flamed out," but neither can we maintain a steady
course without eventually exhausting certain resources. A caveat to head
off the seemingly inevitable flames: yes, I know the "Club of Rome"
forecasts were deeply flawed, and that materials consumption of metals
is actually declining--I've posted analyses on these matters myself.

The problems I'm mainly referring to are of overpopulation in some
areas (please don't cite Julian Simon as proof that the planet is
actually _under_populated!), pollution in some areas, ozone depletion,
loss of habitats, etc. We may disagree as to the extent of the
problems or what the best solutions are.

(Again, to head off the flames which seem to happen so often lately,
let me remind readers that I'm a free market advocate, favor nuclear
power as the best current solution to the greenhouse gases problem,
and am generally a supporter of advanced technologies.)

It's apparent that we cannot "go back" to a simpler set of
technologies without severe consequences, including mass starvation,
more wars over territory, and decreased lifespan. The earth-conscious
hippie slogan, "Split wood, not atoms!" is made all the more absurd
by the growing bans on woodstove and fireplace use!

So what's the "power dive" all about?

We've passed the point of no return, to mix metaphors, and now have no
choice but to proceed as quickly as possible to the newer technologies
which may get us out of the problems we're in now. (Or at least the
putative problems, such as with greenhouse gases and ozone
depletion...I'm not an environmental scientist, and even they mostly
are confused. But since the stakes are so high, errors are serious.
This is, by the way, a good argument for nuclear power--no series of
accidents with nuclear will alter the overall planetary balance, but
continued widespread burning of carbon just _may_. I hear even the
Sierra Club and such are waking up to this truth.)

Proceeding at the current pace does not seem to be a stable option.
Long term, we either cut back our industrialization or proceed to the
next step of technology.

We need to proceed with all haste (the "dive") to ensure our bountiful
future.

Comments?

-Tim

