Date: Sat, 09 Jan 93 00:12:41 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Where are the aliens? (Was How Much of the Extropian Filter have we passed?)

Dani Eder, in an interesting post that's made rethink my ideas on
aliens, has applied stats to the question.  He used a standard deviation
(sigma or z value) of a billion years, based on preceding stellar
generations seeding the universe with metals etc.  And I'll stick with
Dani's guess of a million civilisations per galaxy, if they don't
interact with eachother.  (This figure is less important than the value
for sigma.)

According to my stats tables, and assuming a normal distribution across
time, then the first intragalactic space-faring civilisation precedes
the second by .15z, or 150 million years (roughly what Dani got).  Thus,
as others have argued, we don't expect to meet any aliens native to the
Milky Way.  Thank Goodness.  I still remember the Tasmans.

Applying this to extragalactic civilisations I believe that Dani errs
in concluding that

> by the above statistical argument, the first civilization 
> in this volume [of 500,000 galaxies] should precede the first one in
> our galaxy by 2.2 billion years, which is plenty of time to fill the
> [500 million light year radius] volume at sublight speeds.

Dani has assumed that we are a typical galaxy, and are therefore
somewhere around the 250,000-500,000th civilisation to appear, 2.2
billion years (2.2z) after the first civilisation in the volume.  Hence
we would not exist, since our niche would have been filled back in the
precambrian.  Therefore we are the first?  I'm not convinced.  Let's
look at the time difference between the first and second civilisations
in the total volume appearing.  For about a trillion potential
civilisations in half a million galaxies the first precedes the second
by 'only' about 110 million (.11z) years.  i.e the first civilisation
does not have time to completely expand though the volume before more
civiisations start popping up.  Within a 500 million light year radius
we would expect several civilisations to appear and expand before
crowding out starts.

We could be anyone of these early starters.  And therefore an untypical
galaxy.  But chances are we are *not* the first, unless the area under
the normal curve is *very* low.  We should 'expect' (in probabilistic
terms) the nearest aliens to be about 100 million light years distant
and closing.  Far enough away not to be an immediate concern.  Although
we wouldn't get scores of millions of years warning of their presence,
since intergalactic colonisation / restructuring is probably spreading
at near c.

