Thanks to everyone who responded to my little rant!

I'm disappointed that so many of you seemed to miss the (meta?) point of
my posting.  However I did receive several private emails that lead me to
believe that I got some amount of content out.  I apologize to the rest of
you for misleading you.

Please do not misunderstand me.  I am indeed symathetic to the extropian
memes.  I'm a member of ExI, have read all the back issues of Extropy,
most of the relevant books, and have a 30Mb archive of the postings from this
list.  There is indeed a wealth of great ideas and discussion on this list.

My article was intended to raise questions, stimulate "what if" discussion,
and introduce new sources of ideas.  Very few opinions were expressed.
In fact, I did not pigeon-hole anyone, nor do I make any assumptions as to
the readership of this list.  I assume it is a mosaic of personalities.  It
is you who have made assumptions about my philosophies and views.

Almost every point on my 12 point list was a QUESTION, a PROPOSITION, a
leaping off point for discussion.  I had no idea that I would be barraged
by people who take questions as an expression of opinion.  Please open
your minds.

Most of my "what if" scenarios were answered with knee-jerk reactions assuming
that these were an expression of my opinions.  These are all issues that
need to be discussed and could lead to some interesting debate.  I only
hope that they will be reread with that intent in mind.

-----------
Oblig extropian content (above was meta-noise):

I've been a big fan of extropianism and have spread the memes to numerous
people here at Apple and elsewhere.  I have voraciously read the related
literature.  However, there is a wealth of information available in your
local bookstore that is of interest when building a personal philosophy
and when architecting the future.  Much of it was written some 20, 30, 100,
even 2000 years ago.

My attempt was to bring some of those ideas into this discussion group.
My interests are in TRANSITION STRATEGIES.  How to get from today to the
world that we envision.  More input into our minds means better output.

Let me follow-up on a couple of my points, then introduce a few more....

A.  Redress to history.  The point here was that appeal to modern history
(ie. history of weapons of mass destruction) is incoherent when discussing
long term evolution on the order of tens or hundreds of thousands of years.

Fun quote: European's think that 100 miles is a long distance, Americans
think that 100 years is a long time.

B.  The point about corporations and governments being technologies that
we don't know how to control is rooted in this: they are doing such a
slipshod job of it, and yet we haven't come up with a better way of
managing technologies, societies, and civilizations.

Large corporations are run primarily for the good of the shareholders.
At the top levels, this is what all the decisions are based upon.  At the
lower levels, where quality, ethics, vision exist, the people have little
say in the direction of a company.  They can't communicate effectively due
to large-scale hiearchical organization.  This is why we are seeing the
decay of large corporations such as IBM and General Motors.  The government
is even worse.  These are the largest statist organizations in the world,
and they intend to get even bigger.  They may crush people like us, and
make it impossible to compete in the meme-space.

Last night I watched the confirmation hearings for congressman Aspin for
Director of Security.  It gave me a real insight into how the world's military
might is managed.  Mostly through fear, ignorance, misunderstanding, and
by economic pressure from large corporations who have a big stake in the
game.  This is scary stuff.

C.  About doing something.  This was more of a call to arms.  While
technological and pseudo-social discussion is extremely stimulating, to
actually achieve any of these goals we must put a PLAN into action now.
What is the plan for disolving corporate America?  What is the plan for
usurping the corporate controlled mass media?  What is the plan for
putting a spontaneous order in place of the existing governments?  How
will these plans be disseminated?  Naturally, these 'plans' are more a
set of ideals, of goals.  I'm not advocating some kind of central planning
committee.

D.  The third world.  Oops.  I guess I displayed some ethical concern here.
But seriously, to put it in extropian terms, the concern over the third
world and how we can transform it is rooted in fear that they can stop
us and our "boundless expansion".

I don't worry about the first world blowing itself up.  We've got too
much to lose!  The third world, however, is obtaining technology that
can do the job.  The worry is that they don't have the social context
that we have, therefore may not make the same rational decision to not
use those weapons.

A few well placed nukes/bioweapons/nanomachines in the hands
of a mad man could end it all.  How to prevent this is one of the most
pressing problems of our times.  The question here is, how can extropianism
and the ideas presented in this group be used to remedy this situation.

Perhaps the key concept to grasp is really a question:  Maybe the bounds
to expansion are rooted in the way that a society manages itself and the
gap between rich and poor.  You see, if we become totally techno-entranced,
without taking into account the effects of our actions on the rest of
the population, maybe the system will fall apart before we ever get out
of the solar system.

E.  Global awareness through experience.  The point was this... you can't
fully understand orgasm without having one.  You can't understand an
acid trip without having one.  You can't understand the loss of a lifelong
partner until it has happened to you.  You can't understand uploading until
you've been uploaded.  You can't understand poverty and
starvation without being in its midst.  I haven't been on one of these
voyages, but I have recently met people who have, and it changed their lives.
Hundreds of millions of people are starving.  I'm just starting to
question my own values and priorites, and thought some of you might like
to consider doing the same.

F.  On conciousness.  My myriad of questions weren't meant to be taken
literally!  They set the stage for the meta question which is, since we
don't have a good understanding of conciousness, and we're not particularly
intelligent, then how can we expect to be able to manage technologies that we
develop?  My hypothesis is that our technical abilities outweigh our
psychological abilities.  The development of our technologies have far
outstripped our mental developments.  This causes me concern.  Can we have a
discussion on this issue?


----

In addition to these responses, I'd like to suggest a few more nuggets for
your enjoyment....

1.  Perhaps the all-encompassing rabbit hole is not electronic (ie. VR).
Perhaps it is chemical.  Conceivably a drug could be engineered that
was so great, so illuminating, so entertaining, that it would spread
rapdily through a population.  Perhaps, like LSD, it is so powerful
that it can be poured into the water supply or sprayed into the air
by some well-meaning zealot, and infect the entire population.  Maybe
then they spend their days in blissful contemplation of their navels,
forgoing exploration and reengineering of the universe?

2.  Could you engineer a chemical drug so that it contained an idea?
Once ingested it makes its way into the brain and builds the required
electro-chemical structure that is the idea.

3.  Hey, why not do that with nanotechnology?  Cross the blood-brain
barrier and rewire people's heads.

4.  This is how the technological regression meme could be spread.  Again, we
are discussing local actions with global effects.  

5.  Wrt technological regression... there is indeed a historical precedent
for it.  Several South American cultures in the last 2 millenia have
apparently withdrawn from technological society and regressed into
a more primitive civilization.  The Mayan and some related desert dwelling
tribes, for instance.

6.  Finally, an idea that's going to draw flack.  In coming up with new
technologies we must devote much of our attention to the current and future
social environment.  We unleashed nuclear weapons on an unsuspecting and
unprepared world, and then put them under the control of politicians.
Who evaluated them as being capable of managing this awesome might?
Nanotechnology seems to be even more promising and challenging.  Are
we prepared for it?  More importantly, is the world prepared for it?
There is a time and place for everything.  Scientists need to take some
responsibility for their actions and inventions and evaluate whether the
time is ripe to make a discovery public.  Maybe as a society we won't
be stable enough to handle this technology for a long time after we
invent it.  * Maybe we should censure ourselves? *

By saying that my comments were simply taken out of "Time" and "Newsweek"
with some profanity thrown in is to miss the point entirely.  These are
the ways that we find our global context (yeah, the media sucks, but it's
all I have).

----

Keep the stimulating discussion coming.  Looking forward to your feedback.

-dj


